WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:03.670
Film Courage: What is the hardest part of
making a movie and why?

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:07.020
Matt Eskandari, Writer/Director/Producer:
That’s an interesting one.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:15.050
I think the hardest part about making a film
is the logistics some time.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:20.980
Those unknowable things that you have no control
over and it doesn’t matter if it’s a hundred

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:26.830
million dollar movie or a $200,000 feature,
there are always going to be things that come

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:31.330
up where do don’t have control over them
right?

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.820
And that’s just the nature of what we do,
we are taking a camera, we are pointing it

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:44.180
at real people in real situations and things
in real life…things happen, right?

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.899
This actor isn’t available.

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:50.149
Things are going wrong, a light is not working,
the sun is setting and we’re losing our

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:51.790
light.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:55.340
The sound isn’t…there are loud sounds
here.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:01.649
There are these unknowable things that are
always coming at you as a director and you

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:06.459
have to be able to filter out the ones that
are going to require your attention and the

00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:09.670
ones that you are going to have to move on
from.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:20.770
I think that’s one of those things that
you can’t really learn, you have to experience

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:28.099
where this has happened to you where the sun
is setting, you have 5 shots to get on your

00:01:28.099 --> 00:01:34.810
shot list, “Darn, okay…how do we get this
scene now where the sun is setting?”

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.420
And that’s where you’re thinking as an
editor and you are saying okay the previous

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:43.280
scene we did this and there is a scene where
our character walked from here to here…you

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.280
know what?

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.560
I don’t really need to get this transition.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.060
Let’s just go straight into him, he’s
coming into the room.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.829
It’s being able to be nimble on your feet
and make those adjustments and those cuts

00:01:55.829 --> 00:01:59.569
in your head so you don’t lose your whole
day.

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:02.450
So if the producer comes up to you and says
“Did you get your scene?”

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:04.710
You can say confidently “We got this scene.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.189
It cuts together, watch it.”

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:11.071
Film Courage: Is that where the monk-like
stoicism comes into play?

00:02:11.071 --> 00:02:16.690
It sounds like it could go either way on this
one?

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:17.690
You could panic.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:18.690
Matt: Definitely.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:24.480
Those are the situations where you have to
not let the outside distractions kind of pull

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.260
you away from what your goal is as a director
which is you’ve got to sit behind the monitor

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:36.400
and this is one word of advice I got when
I was in film school is ultimately the director

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.310
is the ideal audience for films.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.290
When they are sitting behind that monitor
or they are sitting next to the monitor watching

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:57.400
the actors, you’ve got to look at 
the next thing.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.920
As a storyteller you are getting yourself
in that position and you are looking at it

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.920
at that position.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:09.299
So being able to turn off everything around
you (all of the noise) from that moment where

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:14.700
you call action until cut and the little in
between and just being able to look at it

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:21.760
as an ideal audience member and just taking
it all in I think is critical to telling your

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:21.959
story.

